    createStaticVNode('<h1 id="_12-raindrop-sensor" tabindex="-1">12. Raindrop Sensor <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-raindrop-sensor" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12. Raindrop Sensor&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="_12-1-introduction" tabindex="-1">12.1 Introduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-1-introduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.1 Introduction&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Designed to monitor precipitation levels, the raindrop sensor converts detected rainfall into proportional analog signals. The sensor plays an essential role in automated weather stations and environmental monitoring setups. Its sensing traces feature an electroless nickel immersion gold finish, also known as ENIG, boosting corrosion resistance and extending overall operating life.</p><img loading="lazy" height="348" width="614" src="' + _imports_0 + '" class="common_img" style="width:300px;"><h2 id="_12-2-technical-specifications" tabindex="-1">12.2 Technical Specifications <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-2-technical-specifications" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.2 Technical Specifications&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:center;">Parameter</th><th style="text-align:center;">Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Operating voltage</td><td style="text-align:center;">5 V</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Surface finish</td><td style="text-align:center;">ENIG finish</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Connector model</td><td style="text-align:center;">5264-4AW</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Mounting compatibility</td><td style="text-align:center;">LEGO-compatible hole spacing</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:center;">Dimensions</td><td style="text-align:center;">37.6 × 24.6 × 7.7 mm</td></tr></tbody></table><h2 id="_12-3-hands-on-project" tabindex="-1">12.3 Hands-On Project <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-3-hands-on-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.3 Hands-On Project&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="_12-3-1-project-introduction" tabindex="-1">12.3.1 Project Introduction <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-3-1-project-introduction" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.3.1 Project Introduction&quot;">​</a></h3><p>This project demonstrates rainfall monitoring by reading analog values from the raindrop sensor and triggering distinct buzzer tones across preset precipitation thresholds. Applying water droplets to the sensing plate changes the sensor readings and activates tiered audio alerts, demonstrating how conditional thresholds process analog signals.</p><h3 id="_12-3-2-hardware-wiring" tabindex="-1">12.3.2 Hardware Wiring <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-3-2-hardware-wiring" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.3.2 Hardware Wiring&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Connect the raindrop sensor to port <strong>A</strong> of the controller using a 4-pin module cable.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="314" width="1015" src="' + _imports_1 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p></p><p><strong>This sensor supports connection to any port among A, B, C, or D on the controller. Ensure that the port selected in the program matches the physical wiring.</strong></p></div><h3 id="_12-3-3-adding-extensions" tabindex="-1">12.3.3 Adding Extensions <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-3-3-adding-extensions" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.3.3 Adding Extensions&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Select <strong>Rain drop sensor</strong> under the <strong>Input Module</strong> category.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="1040" width="1920" src="' + _imports_2 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><h3 id="_12-3-4-complete-program-and-analysis" tabindex="-1">12.3.4 Complete Program and Analysis <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-3-4-complete-program-and-analysis" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.3.4 Complete Program and Analysis&quot;">​</a></h3><ol><li><p>Inside the main loop, continuously read the raindrop sensor on port A.</p></li><li><p>Multi-branch condition: Read the raindrop sensor on port A to get the analog rainfall value and store it in the variable <code>rainfall</code>.</p><ul><li><p>If rainfall is greater than 70: The buzzer plays note C7.</p></li><li><p>If rainfall is greater than 50: The buzzer plays note C5.</p></li><li><p>If rainfall is greater than 30: The buzzer plays note C3.</p></li><li><p>If rainfall is 30 or below: The buzzer turns off, stopping sound output.</p></li></ul></li></ol><img loading="lazy" height="643" width="639" src="' + _imports_3 + '" class="common_img" style="width:500px;"><div class="note custom-block github-alert"><p class="custom-block-title">NOTE</p><p></p><p><strong>The source files are available for download under <a href="https://drive.google.com/drive/folders/1fLfLXEVjSMZdojOq7C44xJqZIBm4MDem?usp=sharing" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Program Files/ 12 Raindrop Sensor.sb3</a>.</strong></p></div><h3 id="_12-3-5-program-upload" tabindex="-1">12.3.5 Program Upload <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-3-5-program-upload" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.3.5 Program Upload&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Click <strong>Connect device</strong>, select the serial port, then click the upload button. Wait for the upload success notification to complete the program transfer.</li></ul><img loading="lazy" height="360" width="640" src="' + _imports_4 + '" class="common_img" style="width:800px;"><h3 id="_12-3-6-program-outcome" tabindex="-1">12.3.6 Program Outcome <a class="header-anchor" href="#_12-3-6-program-outcome" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;12.3.6 Program Outcome&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Once the program upload is complete, apply water droplets to the surface of the raindrop sensor. When the surface remains dry, the buzzer stays silent. Adding light droplets pushes the rainfall value above 30, prompting the buzzer to sound note C3. As moisture increases past 50, the tone shifts to note C5. Heavy droplets exceeding 70 trigger the high-pitched note C7. Wiping the sensing plate clean immediately silences the buzzer.</p>', 25)
